WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.075
Numeric input from files, protocols, command lines, environment variables, configuration, APIs, databases, and user interfaces should be treated as

2
00:00:07.075 --> 00:00:09.681
untrusted until it is parsed and validated.

3
00:00:09.681 --> 00:00:16.011
The fact that a field contains digits does not prove that it is meaningful for the program.

4
00:00:16.011 --> 00:00:20.852
The value still needs syntax checks, range checks, unit checks, and domain checks.

5
00:00:20.852 --> 00:00:29.788
A useful pattern is to parse into a type large enough to evaluate the value safely, then validate before converting into the application type.

6
00:00:29.788 --> 00:00:39.097
For example, an external field might be parsed into a wider signed type so the code can detect negative values, overly large values, empty fields,

7
00:00:39.097 --> 00:00:42.448
malformed text, and unsupported units before narrowing the result.

8
00:00:42.448 --> 00:00:46.171
Validation should reject values that do not fit the domain.

9
00:00:46.171 --> 00:00:53.618
That includes negative counts, unexpectedly huge limits, numbers with the wrong unit, missing fields, duplicated fields, partially parsed values, and

10
00:00:53.618 --> 00:00:57.342
values that are individually valid but inconsistent with related fields.

11
00:00:57.342 --> 00:01:03.300
The correct maximum is usually an application rule, not simply the maximum of the C++ type.

12
00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:05.534
Separate parsing, validation, conversion, and use.

13
00:01:05.534 --> 00:01:11.864
When those steps are mixed together, it becomes harder to see which assumptions have already been checked.

14
00:01:11.864 --> 00:01:20.800
A clean sequence makes it easier to test failures, log useful diagnostics, and avoid using a partly validated value in a memory-sensitive calculation.
